#import "/shared/book.typ": *
#let p = problem.with(code: "MM8036", unit: "U6")
#let items = (
p(1,"E","U6.1 Addition computation",[With addition recursive in its first argument, compute $"add"("suc"("zero"),n)$.],[Use successor, then zero computation.],[Keep the variable $n$ untouched.],[It reduces to $"suc"("add"("zero",n))$, then to $"suc"(n)$. No inspection of $n$ is necessary.],[Show both computation steps.]),
p(2,"E","U6.1 Induction motive",[State the motive for proving right-zero addition.],[The statement must vary with the induction input.],[Use an identity type indexed by $n$.],[The motive is $P(n)="Id"_"Nat"("add"(n,"zero"),n)$. At zero it reduces to reflexive equality; at successor the induction hypothesis can be lifted by congruence of successor.],[State a family of types rather than a single fixed goal.]),
p(3,"E","U6.2 Transport typing",[For $p:"Id"_A(a,b)$ and $u:B(a)$, state the type of $"transport"_B(p,u)$ and its reflexivity computation.],[It moves evidence to the endpoint fiber.],[At reflexivity the endpoints coincide.],[The result has type $B(b)$. When $p$ is $"refl"_a$, transport computes to $u:B(a)$.],[Specify direction from $a$ to $b$.]),
p(4,"E","U6.2 Universe levels",[If $A:U_1$ and $B(x):U_0$, find a universe containing $Pi_(x:A)B(x)$ and one containing $"Id"_A(a,b)$.],[Use maximum for Pi and the base level for identity.],[Both depend on the level of $A$.],[Both types belong to $U_1$: the Pi rule gives $max(1,0)=1$, and identity on a type in $U_1$ stays in $U_1$. No cumulativity is needed for these formation judgments.],[Use the stated noncumulative conventions.]),
p(5,"C","U6.1 Associativity of addition",[Prove addition associative by induction on its first argument.],[Write the motive with the other two arguments fixed.],[Lift the induction equality through successor.],[For fixed $n,k$, induct on $m$ with motive $"Id"("add"("add"(m,n),k),"add"(m,"add"(n,k)))$. At zero both sides compute to $"add"(n,k)$, so reflexivity suffices. At successor both sides compute to the successor of the respective sides for $m$. Apply congruence of successor to the induction hypothesis.],[Reduce both sides before selecting the equality proof.]),
p(6,"C","U6.1 Vector append",[Describe and type both clauses of append on length-indexed vectors.],[Recurse on the first vector, matching the recursion of addition.],[The nil case length converts from $0+n$ to $n$.],[Append has type $"Vec"(A,m)->"Vec"(A,n)->"Vec"(A,"add"(m,n))$. At nil, return the second vector. At $"cons"(a,v)$, return $"cons"(a,"append"(v,w))$. The recursive result has length $"add"(m,n)$ for the tail length $m$, so the constructor has length $"suc"("add"(m,n))$, judgmentally equal to $"add"("suc"(m),n)$.],[Check index conversion in both clauses.]),
p(7,"C","U6.2 Symmetry by J",[Derive symmetry of identity with an explicit motive and reflexive case.],[The desired output reverses endpoints.],[Set $C(a,b,p)="Id"_A(b,a)$.],[Use the motive $C(a,b,p)="Id"_A(b,a)$. Path induction with this motive reduces the construction to $C(a,a,"refl"_a)="Id"_A(a,a)$. Supply $"refl"_a$. The eliminator then defines symmetry for every $p:"Id"_A(a,b)$ and computes to reflexivity on a reflexive input.],[Allow the endpoint $b$ to vary in the motive.]),
p(8,"C","U6.2 Congruence",[For $f:A->D$, derive $"Id"_A(a,b)->"Id"_D(f(a),f(b))$. Why is this an implication about identity proofs rather than an equality-reflection rule?],[Use path induction on the input proof.],[The reflexive case is reflexivity at $f(a)$.],[Take motive $C(a,b,p)="Id"_D(f(a),f(b))$ and reflexive data $"refl"_(f(a))$. J supplies the function. Its result is a term of an identity type; it does not add the conversion judgment $f(a) equiv f(b)$ for an arbitrary variable $p$.],[Distinguish an internal proof from a judgmental conversion.]),
p(9,"C","U6.1 Safe head",[Explain why a head function $"Vec"(A,"suc"(n))->A$ needs no nil branch. Can the same total signature be given for all lengths without extra data?],[Constructor indices constrain which inputs can inhabit the domain.],[Nil has length zero.],[Only a cons constructor can inhabit a successor-length vector in this inductive family, so head returns its first component. A function on all lengths would also have to handle nil; with arbitrary $A$ there may be no element to return, so such a uniform total head needs a nonempty-length condition, a default element, or an optional result type.],[Use the index, not an unchecked runtime assumption.]),
p(10,"A","U6.2 Transport composition",[For $p:"Id"_A(a,b)$ and $q:"Id"_A(b,c)$, prove transport along their composite agrees propositionally with successive transport on $u:B(a)$.],[Induct on the paths, generalizing their endpoints appropriately.],[After both become reflexivity, both expressions compute to $u$.],[Use path induction on $p$ and then $q$, with the target family asserting equality in $B(c)$ between $"transport"_B("trans"(p,q),u)$ and $"transport"_B(q,"transport"_B(p,u))$. Generalize the varying endpoints and retain $u$ in its proper starting fiber. At $p=q="refl"$, transitivity and both transports reduce, leaving $"Id"_(B(a))(u,u)$, inhabited by reflexivity. J lifts this base case to all endpoints and paths.],[State the common result fiber and do not assume general paths compute.]),
p(11,"A","U6.2 Judgmental and propositional equality",[Compare the left-zero and right-zero laws for addition. Explain why a proof of right-zero does not make its open expression reduce by definition.],[Inspect which argument the recursive definition matches.],[A variable in the first argument blocks computation.],[Left-zero $"add"("zero",n) equiv n$ follows directly by the defining equation. Right-zero for variable $n$ requires induction and produces a term of $"Id"("add"(n,"zero"),n)$. The recursive definition still cannot select a constructor of a variable, so the expression remains stuck under judgmental reduction. Equality elimination may transport along the proof, but equality reflection is not a rule of this theory.],[Keep reduction, propositional proof, and transport distinct.]),
p(12,"A","U6.2 Diagnosing misuse of path induction",[A student argues: “Apply J to $p:"Id"_A(a,a)$, so every such $p$ equals reflexivity.” Identify the missing motive issue; explain what J actually supplies.],[J varies an endpoint before specializing to loops.],[The alleged comparison to reflexivity is not well typed when the endpoint varies.],[In based path induction one must define a motive for arbitrary $b:A$ and $p:"Id"_A(a,b)$. The proposed equality between $p$ and $"refl"_a$ is not an identity type in one common fiber for arbitrary $b$, because reflexivity inhabits $"Id"_A(a,a)$ rather than $"Id"_A(a,b)$. Restricting the motive to $b=a$ before invoking J changes the eliminator. J permits valid families over all such endpoints, yielding transport and congruence; it does not by this argument yield the stronger K/UIP principle, which we have not assumed.],[Identify the exact ill-typed comparison rather than merely naming UIP.]),
)
